*Same fader, three different spots.*

## The board disappeared

A few days later I went to use it and decibulb couldn't find the R24 at all. `lsusb` didn't list it, and the Pi's log had this in it.

```
hwmon hwmon3: Undervoltage detected!
```

The Pi dipped in power for a couple of seconds right when the R24 was trying to start, so the R24 never finished turning on. The `usb_max_current_enable` line was still there, but this Pi is also running a bunch of Docker containers now, so there's less power left over for the R24 than when I first got it working. It was the power again.

I fixed it by switching the Pi off PoE and onto a full power cable. That gives the Pi more power to work with, so it has enough left over for the R24 too. The undervoltage warnings went away and the R24 showed up again.

I also wrote this up in the README, with the options from best to worst: the board's own adapter, a powered USB hub, batteries, and last, the Pi's USB port. A setup that works one week can stop working the next just because you added something else to the Pi.

## Making it reliable

At this point decibulb worked, but only if everything was perfect when I started it, so I fixed the things that could go wrong.

Right after connecting, it now asks every bulb whether it's on. If a lamp is off, moving its fader turns it on first, the master fader skips it and the on/off button works on the first press.

It also skips commands that don't change anything. The bulbs round everything, and brightness only goes in whole percents, so 50.1% and 50.4% look exactly the same. Since every command uses up 0.3 seconds now, sending one that doesn't change anything just makes the next real change wait. So decibulb works out what the bulb would actually show, and if the bulb is already showing that, it doesn't send it.

Before, if the R24 was off when decibulb started, it would just quit. And if I turned the R24 off while it was running, decibulb would sit there forever, because a board that turns off doesn't send an error, it just goes quiet. Now it waits for the board to show up, notices when it disappears and picks it back up when it comes back.

If a lamp is off at the wall when decibulb starts, it tries again every 30 seconds, and once the lamp shows up its fader just starts working. I didn't make it faster than 30 seconds because searching for a lamp uses the same Bluetooth radio the other lamps are using.

All of that was needed for the last piece, which was running it on boot. decibulb is set up as a service now, so it starts when the Pi turns on and restarts itself if anything crashes, and I don't have to SSH in to start it.

### Lamps that drop out

A couple of days later the board stopped doing anything, and the log was full of this, over and over.

```
desk_lamp: connect failed: ... device not found: The device disappeared
```

The board was fine. Two of the lamps had dropped off Bluetooth while decibulb was running, and decibulb kept sending every fader move to the dead connection. The Bluetooth library tries to reconnect on every command, but it never searches for the bulb again first, and once Linux forgets a bulb, nothing can reconnect to it without a new search.

The startup retry already did that search, it just only ran for lamps that were missing when decibulb started. So now if a lamp fails twice in a row, decibulb gives up on that connection and hands the lamp to the same retry. It searches every 30 seconds until the lamp shows back up, and the lamp even keeps the fader level it had before it dropped.

## v0.1.0

After that I cleaned it up so other people can actually use it.

Every push to GitHub now runs all 137 tests on a fresh machine on three versions of Python, so if I break something I find out right away. None of the tests need the real mixer or bulbs, which is what makes that possible.

I also wrote a proper README that covers how to set it up, what each part does and everything I learned about the R24 and the bulbs the hard way, like the power problems and why it only sends 3 changes a second. It also says you don't need a Pi, since any Linux computer with Bluetooth works.

And it's tagged as v0.1.0 on GitHub under the MIT license. It's up at [github.com/agulam-coco/decibulb](https://github.com/agulam-coco/decibulb).

Next is the room volume, where Alexa plays through the Pi, out of the R24 and into the Rockville, and one fader sets how loud it is.